1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,890
In this video,
we're going to talk

2
00:00:01,890 --> 00:00:03,945
about building the Linux kernel.

3
00:00:03,945 --> 00:00:06,480
We'll do an introduction
to KConfig,

4
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:08,790
talk about kernel
defconfigs and go through

5
00:00:08,790 --> 00:00:10,050
the steps that we take to build

6
00:00:10,050 --> 00:00:12,070
the Linux kernel from source.

7
00:00:12,070 --> 00:00:14,850
We'll use a QEMU example kernel

8
00:00:14,850 --> 00:00:16,320
build that will be part of

9
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:18,970
what you use for
assignment number 3.

10
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:23,045
First we talk about
the KConfig format.

11
00:00:23,045 --> 00:00:26,810
This is a popular format
used for Linux development,

12
00:00:26,810 --> 00:00:28,370
especially with the
kernel, but also

13
00:00:28,370 --> 00:00:30,245
with utilities like BusyBox.

14
00:00:30,245 --> 00:00:34,540
This is a text format that
controls kernel configuration.

15
00:00:34,540 --> 00:00:36,900
Here's an example of
what it looks like.

16
00:00:36,900 --> 00:00:38,295
Pretty easy to read.

17
00:00:38,295 --> 00:00:42,395
There's a item describing a
menu item and then a config

18
00:00:42,395 --> 00:00:47,220
label that describes a
specific configuration.

19
00:00:47,220 --> 00:00:48,920
Like in this case, it's talking

20
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:52,050
about device memory
virtual support.

21
00:00:52,250 --> 00:00:56,600
The type here defined is
Boolean, the default value,

22
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:59,840
and some help that can
be used by a program to

23
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:05,100
show what this value does
when it's turned on or off.

24
00:01:05,780 --> 00:01:08,450
The graphical tool that

25
00:01:08,450 --> 00:01:10,520
usually parses these
KConfig files,

26
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:11,720
there are a few
different options,

27
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:14,195
but one of them is
called menuconfig.

28
00:01:14,195 --> 00:01:17,760
That's a terminal based
utility that you could

29
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:21,230
use if you just type make
menuconfig in the kernel tree,

30
00:01:21,230 --> 00:01:23,630
you'll see a utility
like this that lets you

31
00:01:23,630 --> 00:01:26,510
use the space bar
to go select one

32
00:01:26,510 --> 00:01:29,030
or more of the configurations

33
00:01:29,030 --> 00:01:31,550
for the kernel and you
can see a map here

34
00:01:31,550 --> 00:01:34,280
between the KConfig text file

35
00:01:34,280 --> 00:01:37,945
and what shows up here
in the menuconfig tool.

36
00:01:37,945 --> 00:01:39,960
This text file
essentially just drives

37
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:43,300
the content of the
menuconfig tool.

38
00:01:43,310 --> 00:01:46,265
Once we make changes
with menuconfig,

39
00:01:46,265 --> 00:01:48,100
our changes are going
to be stored in

40
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:50,995
a.config file at the kernel
source root directory.

41
00:01:50,995 --> 00:01:54,610
That.config file won't show

42
00:01:54,610 --> 00:01:57,345
up with the ls because
it's a hidden file,

43
00:01:57,345 --> 00:02:00,160
but you'll see it if you
search for it specifically,

44
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:03,760
or if you use the
dash a option for ls.

45
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:06,400
Using the example that
we showed before of

46
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:08,525
that DEVMEM character device,

47
00:02:08,525 --> 00:02:11,635
if we turn that on inside
the kernel config,

48
00:02:11,635 --> 00:02:13,300
we'll see an entry in our.config

49
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:15,860
file that says CONFIG_DEVMEM=y.

50
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:19,930
This is setting the Boolean
true to say we want to

51
00:02:19,930 --> 00:02:23,830
turn on character device
support in our kernel build.

52
00:02:25,250 --> 00:02:29,600
For most of the kernel drivers,

53
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:31,190
there are a few
different options you

54
00:02:31,190 --> 00:02:33,270
can select at KConfig.

55
00:02:33,270 --> 00:02:34,850
The first is off

56
00:02:34,850 --> 00:02:37,100
saying that you're not
going to build and it's not

57
00:02:37,100 --> 00:02:38,480
going to be included in
your kernel and you can

58
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:40,010
see this in menuconfig with this

59
00:02:40,010 --> 00:02:43,715
empty not selected for you.

60
00:02:43,715 --> 00:02:45,365
The second is on.

61
00:02:45,365 --> 00:02:47,900
When it's on, it's going to
be built into the kernel

62
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:51,470
and no need to load
it at runtime,

63
00:02:51,470 --> 00:02:52,970
it'll just be directly built in,

64
00:02:52,970 --> 00:02:55,900
and you can notice that
with the asterisk here.

65
00:02:55,900 --> 00:02:58,425
Then the final is the module.

66
00:02:58,425 --> 00:03:01,485
In this case, it's going
to be loadable at runtime.

67
00:03:01,485 --> 00:03:03,869
You'll be able to use

68
00:03:03,869 --> 00:03:06,980
modprobe or other
commands to load it

69
00:03:06,980 --> 00:03:09,710
dynamically into the
kernel and that's shown

70
00:03:09,710 --> 00:03:13,620
by this m here in
the menuconfig view.

71
00:03:13,870 --> 00:03:19,310
Second thing to know about
kernel configs is that

72
00:03:19,310 --> 00:03:21,140
you want to start based on

73
00:03:21,140 --> 00:03:22,580
whatever machine you're using

74
00:03:22,580 --> 00:03:24,080
or whatever architecture
you're using,

75
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:25,430
you typically want to start with

76
00:03:25,430 --> 00:03:27,755
a default configuration
for your kernel.

77
00:03:27,755 --> 00:03:30,570
That's often referred to
as a kernel defconfig.

78
00:03:30,570 --> 00:03:32,360
This is really just a
file that sets up your

79
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:34,460
initial.config to have a set

80
00:03:34,460 --> 00:03:36,020
of known defaults that work with

81
00:03:36,020 --> 00:03:38,480
a specific hardware
platform and typically,

82
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:39,920
this is something that you get

83
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:41,690
like four-year
development board,

84
00:03:41,690 --> 00:03:45,020
a base configuration setup
for you and you'd use

85
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:49,630
this as the basis for your
own custom kernel.config.

86
00:03:49,630 --> 00:03:53,720
In our case, we're going to
do something a little bit

87
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:55,400
different than what's
discussed in Mastering

88
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:57,455
Embedded Linux
Programming Chapter 4,

89
00:03:57,455 --> 00:03:59,855
and the reason is we're
going to take advantage of

90
00:03:59,855 --> 00:04:02,540
the arm cross-compiler
tools that

91
00:04:02,540 --> 00:04:05,135
we used with
Assignment 2, and so,

92
00:04:05,135 --> 00:04:08,580
instead of using
the instructions

93
00:04:08,580 --> 00:04:11,505
in Mastering Embedded Linux
Programming Chapter 4,

94
00:04:11,505 --> 00:04:14,610
we're going to use
the QEMU virt target.

95
00:04:14,610 --> 00:04:17,580
That, you can specify it with

96
00:04:17,580 --> 00:04:20,910
the virt machine
target defconfig,

97
00:04:20,910 --> 00:04:23,120
or you could just
make defconfig with

98
00:04:23,120 --> 00:04:27,935
no arguments to use this
default QEMU virtual machine.

99
00:04:27,935 --> 00:04:30,425
There are a bunch more
options that you can use,

100
00:04:30,425 --> 00:04:32,030
and you can find these here

101
00:04:32,030 --> 00:04:36,190
in the Linux source
directory under configs.

102
00:04:36,980 --> 00:04:40,380
We're going to compile with

103
00:04:40,380 --> 00:04:44,590
Kbuild using the
KConfig framework.

104
00:04:44,590 --> 00:04:46,640
The examples that we showed

105
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:48,640
before about built-in or module,

106
00:04:48,640 --> 00:04:51,065
you can see references here to

107
00:04:51,065 --> 00:04:54,245
the object files that are
included with your driver,

108
00:04:54,245 --> 00:04:55,640
and whether they're
going to be built into

109
00:04:55,640 --> 00:04:57,420
the kernel with this -y,

110
00:04:57,420 --> 00:04:59,735
or if they're going
to be selectable,

111
00:04:59,735 --> 00:05:01,490
either built into the kernel,

112
00:05:01,490 --> 00:05:04,325
not built into the kernel
or included as module.

113
00:05:04,325 --> 00:05:08,360
Looking back at what we
did with the.config file,

114
00:05:08,360 --> 00:05:10,190
that reference there to

115
00:05:10,190 --> 00:05:12,470
the config value is

116
00:05:12,470 --> 00:05:14,725
going to just be
filled into the obj-,

117
00:05:14,725 --> 00:05:17,480
and it's going to
essentially build a list of

118
00:05:17,480 --> 00:05:18,860
objects that we're going to

119
00:05:18,860 --> 00:05:21,645
compile as a part of
our kernel build.

120
00:05:21,645 --> 00:05:23,580
You can see here if I don't have

121
00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:27,215
CONFIG_TTY_PRINTK defined as Y,

122
00:05:27,215 --> 00:05:29,765
it's not going to end
up in the obj-y list,

123
00:05:29,765 --> 00:05:33,390
and we're not going to
compile TTY_PRINTK.C.

124
00:05:33,550 --> 00:05:36,485
The other thing you
can see here is that

125
00:05:36,485 --> 00:05:39,545
the mem and random.o

126
00:05:39,545 --> 00:05:45,365
are requirements for the
kernel build of TTY_PRINTK,

127
00:05:45,365 --> 00:05:48,670
and so those are just going
to be set by default.

128
00:05:48,670 --> 00:05:52,100
But the TTY_PRINTK itself
will be turned on or

129
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:55,770
off based on that configuration
in the.config file.

130
00:05:56,320 --> 00:05:58,340
Another thing to talk about are

131
00:05:58,340 --> 00:06:01,165
the build artifacts or
the output of the build.

132
00:06:01,165 --> 00:06:04,205
In our case, we're going to
be using the VM Linux file,

133
00:06:04,205 --> 00:06:05,690
which is an ELF binar.

134
00:06:05,690 --> 00:06:08,645
It's a executable linker format,

135
00:06:08,645 --> 00:06:12,560
a binary file and depending
on how you build the kernel,

136
00:06:12,560 --> 00:06:15,750
this may also include
debugging symbols.

137
00:06:16,280 --> 00:06:20,070
Another set of build artifacts,

138
00:06:20,070 --> 00:06:23,344
underneath the architecture
boot directory,

139
00:06:23,344 --> 00:06:25,595
there'll be some
bootloader files used

140
00:06:25,595 --> 00:06:28,520
if you're going to be
working with hardware.

141
00:06:28,520 --> 00:06:30,410
We'll get into these
later when we get into

142
00:06:30,410 --> 00:06:33,020
the final project
portion of the course.

143
00:06:33,020 --> 00:06:36,890
For this assignment, we won't
need to get into these,

144
00:06:36,890 --> 00:06:40,850
but there's binary version of
VM Linux in the image file,

145
00:06:40,850 --> 00:06:43,430
there's a compressed image
and a compressed image that

146
00:06:43,430 --> 00:06:46,655
specifically meant for use
with U-Boot bootloaders.

147
00:06:46,655 --> 00:06:48,800
For our Assignment
3 build steps,

148
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:51,150
we could start out
with a clean command.

149
00:06:51,150 --> 00:06:53,870
The example would look
like something like this,

150
00:06:53,870 --> 00:06:56,780
where we say make
ARCH equals arm64 and

151
00:06:56,780 --> 00:06:58,280
cross-compile we'd use that same

152
00:06:58,280 --> 00:07:01,115
cross-compile for prefix that
we used for Assignment 2,

153
00:07:01,115 --> 00:07:03,035
and then specify the mrproper.

154
00:07:03,035 --> 00:07:05,465
That's going to deep clean
the kernel build tree,

155
00:07:05,465 --> 00:07:08,765
removing.config file and
any existing configuration.

156
00:07:08,765 --> 00:07:10,490
Next step, we'll
do the defconfig

157
00:07:10,490 --> 00:07:12,730
build and set up
our default config.

158
00:07:12,730 --> 00:07:15,060
This is the same
make ARCH equals

159
00:07:15,060 --> 00:07:18,315
arm64 cross-compile string
that we use previously,

160
00:07:18,315 --> 00:07:20,745
and then we'll use
the target defconfig.

161
00:07:20,745 --> 00:07:22,460
This is going to configure for

162
00:07:22,460 --> 00:07:25,130
our "virt" arm device

163
00:07:25,130 --> 00:07:27,870
that we're going to
simulate in QEMU.

164
00:07:27,950 --> 00:07:30,965
Then we're going to build
our VM Linux target.

165
00:07:30,965 --> 00:07:35,600
If we say make our ARCH equals
arm64 CROSS_COMPILE all.

166
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:40,145
The j4 will just allow running
multiple compiles at once,

167
00:07:40,145 --> 00:07:43,820
which could speed up things
if you have multiple CPUs on

168
00:07:43,820 --> 00:07:46,250
your system and this
is going to build

169
00:07:46,250 --> 00:07:50,310
our kernel image that we
use for booting QEMU.

170
00:07:51,140 --> 00:07:54,770
Finally, we can build
the modules and

171
00:07:54,770 --> 00:07:58,760
devicetree file
using the same make

172
00:07:58,760 --> 00:08:00,890
commands or the
same make arguments

173
00:08:00,890 --> 00:08:02,660
we used before and using

174
00:08:02,660 --> 00:08:04,640
the targetmodules to
build modules and

175
00:08:04,640 --> 00:08:07,890
building dtbs to
build the devicetree.